# Request context and extensions

Upwell request metadata and Axum extensions solve different problems. <Symbol path="upwell::axum::RequestMeta" /> is seeded by the framework into every Upwell HTTP request scope. `axum::extract::Extension<T>` succeeds only when an Axum layer or router inserted `T` into request extensions.

## Read native request metadata through DI

`RequestMeta` contains the method, URI, complete header map, and successfully parsed cookies keyed by name.

<Example title="Build a request-scoped identity">

```rust
use upwell::axum::{RequestMeta, scope::Request};
use upwell::prelude::*;

#[component(scope = Request)]
struct RequestIdentity {
    meta: RequestMeta,
}

impl RequestIdentity {
    fn bearer(&self) -> Option<&str> {
        self.meta
            .headers
            .get("authorization")?
            .to_str()
            .ok()?
            .strip_prefix("Bearer ")
    }
}
```

The value is injectable by value and cloned within one request's bounded metadata. A request-scoped component may depend on singleton state, but a singleton must not retain request-scoped identity.

</Example>

A handler can extract `Inject<Arc<RequestIdentity>>` or the by-value framework seed as `Inject<RequestMeta>`. Failure to resolve either indicates the handler is outside the Upwell-managed HTTP request scope or the defining crate's feature/registration set is inconsistent.

## Install and extract an Axum extension

Use an extension for a value owned by router middleware rather than the Upwell component graph:

```rust
use upwell::axum::axum::Extension;
use upwell::axum::prelude::*;
use upwell::prelude::*;

#[derive(Clone)]
struct DeploymentRegion(&'static str);

#[controller(path = "/deployment")]
struct DeploymentController;

#[handlers]
impl DeploymentController {
    #[get("/region")]
    async fn region(
        &self,
        Extension(region): Extension<DeploymentRegion>,
    ) -> String {
        region.0.to_owned()
    }
}

let app = App::builder("catalog-api")
    .auto_discover()
    .layer(Extension(DeploymentRegion("eu-west")))
    .build()
    .await?;
```

<Symbol path="upwell_axum::plugin::AxumAppBuilder::layer" /> applies a standard Tower/Axum layer to the assembled Upwell router. The generated route keeps <code>Extension&lt;DeploymentRegion&gt;</code> as a server-only extractor, so it does not become an argument on the generated client method. Missing <code>Extension&lt;T&gt;</code> rejects extraction according to Axum's response behavior; it is not a DI construction error.

## Choose the boundary

| Need | Use |
|---|---|
| method, URI, headers, cookies in an Upwell handler/component | `RequestMeta` |
| request-scoped component constructed from framework dependencies | Axum `scope::Request` + DI |
| value inserted by arbitrary Axum middleware/router | `Extension<T>` |
| one handler's ordinary path/query/body input | Axum extractor |
| WebSocket connection identity after upgrade | connection-scope metadata, not retained HTTP request scope |

<Callout type="warning" title="Do not bridge by global mutable state">

If middleware data must enter DI, install a deliberate scope seed or component integration. Copying request values into a singleton or task-local with unclear cleanup breaks concurrency and WebSocket upgrades.

</Callout>

## WebSocket inheritance

An accepted upgrade creates an Axum `upwell::axum::scope::Connection` scope under the root, then a child `upwell::axum::scope::Request` scope for each decoded application message. The framework seeds `WebsocketUpgradeMeta` into the connection scope, so message-scoped dependencies resolve the upgrade method, URI, headers, and cookies through the parent chain. <Symbol path="upwell_axum::ws::WsConnectionMeta" /> separately exposes the selected RFC 6455 subprotocol.

This is deliberate metadata inheritance, not retention of the original HTTP request scope. Put longer-lived derived authentication state in a connection-scoped component and keep per-message state request-scoped.

## Troubleshoot context

- `Extension<T>` missing means no covering Axum layer inserted exactly that `T`.
- `RequestMeta` missing means dispatch did not open the framework HTTP request scope.
- Cookies absent while the header exists may be malformed pairs; parsing intentionally drops invalid cookies.
- A WebSocket message handler may resolve inherited `WebsocketUpgradeMeta`; unrelated HTTP request-scoped component instances are not carried into the socket.
- Custom router composition can place a layer on the wrong side of `merge` or `nest`; inspect which router owns the final route.

See <GuideRef slug="axum/http">HTTP controllers</GuideRef>, <GuideRef slug="axum/websocket">WebSocket protocols</GuideRef>, and <GuideRef slug="framework/dependency-injection/advanced">advanced dependency injection</GuideRef>.
